# 800-APP-0102 — What the shell's `run` takes from the link's client, in developer mode — done

**Type:** tools (`shell`) · **Owner:** tools track (`APP`) · **Priority:** P1 (the maintainer: "you must be able to run commands there", 2026-10-22) · **Status:** done (2026-10-29) · **Blocked by:** — (`KRN`'s client is in `main` with ABI 15, 800-KRN-0148) · **Main task:** [800](../issues/800-mind-link.md) · **Roadmap:** the development of MIND Core on real hardware (as 800) · **Constitution:** MC-3.11, MC-12.1, MC-12.3

**Recorded by:** the tools track, 2026-10-22, from LNK's request (LNK → APP, 2026-10-22 03:56 UTC, for 800-LNK-0015) and the maintainer's choice (a) that LNK passed on at 03:58: "да, режим а".

## Problem

- **What LNK needs:** with `dev` in `link.conf`, an agent at the other end of the cable must run the shell's own commands, such as `ip`, `ps`, `netstat` and `dmesg`. These are not programs, so `link`'s run by name does not reach them.
- **What exists:** `link` will call the shell's `run(line)` (`idl/shell.wit` 1.1) with a client badged as the link's (800-LNK-0015, KRN's part).
- **What does not fit:** `shell/src/clients.rs` asks the user in the shell's window before what changes the machine or reaches beyond it, and over the cable no one is at that window.

## Plan

- **The badge:** `mind::process::BADGE_SHELL_LINK` (1) on the shell's commands endpoint. The clients the shell lends carry no badge and no right to set one, so no program can pass for the link.
- **The endpoint:** the shell made its commands endpoint itself at its first lend, which `init` could not hand to `link`, started before the shell. KRN's 800-KRN-0148 (ABI 15) makes it in `init`:
  - the receive side of the shell's keeper is in `SLOT_COMMANDS` (37);
  - `link`'s client is minted from that keeper with `BADGE_SHELL_LINK`, in `link`'s slot 30, with `dev`.

  The shell serves on `SLOT_COMMANDS` when it holds it, and mints the clients it lends from it. Without it, the shell makes its own as before. (A first version served on the shell's own `SLOT_SHELL`, a proposal KRN replaced.)
- **`clients::taken_by(line, Client::Link)`:**
  - the commands any client gets at once, as before;
  - what a program's client is asked runs at once: the commands that only read (`netstat`, `ping`, `nslookup`, `logs`, `stat`, `pmap`, `caps <pid>`), and, by the maintainer's choice (a), those that change the machine (`kill`, `stop`, `reboot`, `poweroff`, `shutdown`, `budget`, `date set`, `logger`, `tpm`, `fetch`, `https`, a `>` into a file);
  - refused:
    - `netpolicy` with a change and `netrevoke` (800-NET-0038: the link does not change the policy);
    - `mode`;
    - scripts (`msh`);
    - and what is refused to every client: the screen's own commands, programs and statements.
- **No question:** the shell runs the link's line without asking in its window. `reboot`, `poweroff`, `shutdown` and `stop` skip their own question too.
- **The log:** the system log names each command from the link by its word alone, `[SHELL] FOR THE LINK THE SHELL RAN PS`, as `link` logs it, so no argument reaches the log.
- **The answer:** the last 7000 bytes of what the command printed, as for every client. `refused` when the rule refuses.
- **`dmesg` is a program,** not a shell command. The developer mode already runs it by name (800-LNK-0012).

## Acceptance criteria

- **A host test** (`tests/shell_host.rs`): the link's client gets at once what a program's client is asked, and is refused the policy's changes, `mode` and scripts. A program's client is still asked.
- **The QEMU `link` suite with `dev`** (LNK's, once KRN's client exists): `sh ip`, `sh ps` and a refusal.
- **Docs:** `docs/tools/README.md` and `README_RU.md`.

What runs over the link is evidence for the configurations tested only (MC-12.1). The shell decides what the link's client may run. This task claims nothing for a client that `init` does not mint (MC-12.3).

## On the MacBook Pro (2026-10-22)

- **The link's commands answer.** LNK reported it (`APP/2026-10-22T1555Z-…`, `ALL/2026-10-22T1552Z-…`): over the link, `sh ip`, `sh netstat -i` and `sh ps` answered.
  - The release was b509a30, built from `fast-test`, which carried this task's shell.
  - It is evidence for that machine and that release only (MC-12.1).
- **Left:** the `link` suite's `sh` checks, which LNK keeps skipped until this task is in `main`.

## Related

[800-LNK-0012](../issues-done/800-LNK-0012-the-developer-mode.done), [800-KRN-0139](../issues/800-KRN-0139-the-links-developer-mode.md), [211-APP-0044](../issues-done/211-APP-0044-console-joined-to-the-shell.done) (`run`), 800-LNK-0015 (on LNK's branch).

## Resolution

Done (2026-10-29).
- **The shell's part** (`shell/src/clients.rs`, `Client::Link`; `shell/src/main.rs`) has been in `main` since 61df5767. It serves `SLOT_COMMANDS` and takes the line of the client badged `BADGE_SHELL_LINK` as the rule above says.
- **The host test** `the_links_client_gets_at_once_what_a_program_is_asked_but_not_the_policy` in `tests/shell_host.rs` passes.
- **The docs** are in `docs/tools/README.md` and `README_RU.md` (4.8).
- **The QEMU `link` suite with `dev`** ran its `sh` checks with no skip, and every check passed:
  - `sh ip` answered with its cards;
  - `sh ps` answered;
  - `sh netpolicy add …` was refused.

  The run: x86, 4 CPUs, TCG, on the tools branch's 10d7b849 (`main` ced25026 merged), with `--suites tools,services,link`. It is evidence for that configuration only (MC-12.1).
- **On the MacBook Pro,** LNK reported `sh ip`, `sh netstat -i` and `sh ps` answering over the link on 2026-10-22 (above).
